0
00:00:00,720 --> 00:00:07,880
Hello everyone, the title of this presentation is "TCLNet: Learning to Locate Typhoon Center Using Deep Neural Network".

1
00:00:08,680 --> 00:00:12,320
And my name is Chao Tan, from Chongqing University of Technology.

2
00:00:14,150 --> 00:00:18,430
The task of typhoon center location plays an important role in typhoon analysis.

3
00:00:19,490 --> 00:00:26,290
In this work, we use a single frame infrared satellite cloud imagery to determine the position of typhoon eye or spin center.

4
00:00:27,180 --> 00:00:30,220
The applications of typhoon center location are wide ranging.

5
00:00:30,910 --> 00:00:37,110
For example, typhoon intensity estimation, typhoon path forecasting and typhoon center tracking.

6
00:00:38,560 --> 00:00:41,960
In this work, we try to use a deep learning network to solve this problem.

7
00:00:43,050 --> 00:00:48,850
Therefore, we first propose a brand-new large-scale typhoon center location dataset named TCLD,

8
00:00:49,690 --> 00:00:55,770
which contains more than 1800 training samples and 300 testing samples from meteorological satellite.

9
00:00:56,630 --> 00:01:02,430
In addition, we use the mean location error (MLE) to measure the performance of the algorithm on the TCLD dataset.

10
00:01:03,450 --> 00:01:08,930
MLE calculates the coordinate distance between the predicted typhoon center and the corresponding label.

11
00:01:09,660 --> 00:01:16,900
We also reported the MLE of human experience on TCLD dataset, from this, we can see that compared with the eyed typhoon,

12
00:01:17,460 --> 00:01:20,260
the center locating error of the non-eyed typhoon is greater.

13
00:01:21,750 --> 00:01:27,190
The main components of our typhoon center location network TCLNet are ConvBlock and ResBlock.

14
00:01:28,120 --> 00:01:32,920
The overall structure of TCLNet is a fully convolutional encoding-decoding network.

15
00:01:33,730 --> 00:01:39,290
It contains a preprocessing part for feature preprocessing and encode-decode part for feature transformation.

16
00:01:40,290 --> 00:01:46,330
In addition, TCLNet generated the typhoon center coordinate heatmaps and trained using mean square error. 

17
00:01:47,300 --> 00:01:52,060
In the testing phase, we obtained the final typhoon center position through the coordinate heatmap.

18
00:01:53,450 --> 00:02:02,450
In terms of experimentation, we conduct experiments on ResNet, SimpleBaseline, Feature Pyramid Network (FPN), Cascaded Pyramid Network (CPN) and Hourglass Network.

19
00:02:03,210 --> 00:02:10,010
It can be seen that compare with the current best method, our TCLNet achieves a 14.4% increase in accuracy

20
00:02:10,540 --> 00:02:14,300
on the basis of a 92.7% reduction in model parameters.

21
00:02:15,920 --> 00:02:17,960
In the process of training TCLNet,

22
00:02:18,360 --> 00:02:26,760
we observed that the model has large differences in Mean Square Error values for different samples in the course of the experiments, and the loss distribution is long-tailed.

23
00:02:27,730 --> 00:02:36,490
Due to the training dataset contains about thirty-five percent non-eyed typhoon samples, and these hard samples contribute a large loss value during the training process,

24
00:02:36,990 --> 00:02:39,710
thus affect the model to optimize in the correct direction.

25
00:02:40,530 --> 00:02:46,570
To solve this problem, we propose a new loss function TCL+ for the task of typhoon center location.

26
00:02:47,600 --> 00:02:53,680
TCL+ loss suppress the loss of hard samples in the training process, and instead learning it through easy samples.

27
00:02:54,620 --> 00:02:58,300
We compare the effects of mean square error loss and TCL+ loss,

28
00:02:58,870 --> 00:03:04,950
and the result shows that training with our TCL+ loss can achieve better performance than traditional mean square error loss.

29
00:03:06,410 --> 00:03:09,330
We also visualize some test results of TCLNet.

30
00:03:09,840 --> 00:03:15,840
It can be seen that TCLNet achieves comparable results on eyed typhoon samples compare with manual labels,

31
00:03:16,200 --> 00:03:19,600
and also outputs reasonable results on non-eyed typhoon samples.

32
00:03:21,260 --> 00:03:28,300
Our TCLNet follows the lightweight and simplest model architecture and achieves the best performance with the smallest amount of network parameters.

33
00:03:29,340 --> 00:03:35,860
To illustrate this point, we studied the impact of different components and network structures on typhoon center location task.

34
00:03:36,750 --> 00:03:42,790
From the result of the experiment, we can see that the performance of the models with strategies such as using Hourglass network,

35
00:03:43,260 --> 00:03:48,020
increasing the downsampling scales and using skip connection is not as good as our TCLNet.

36
00:03:49,160 --> 00:03:53,560
Meanwhile, the complex network architecture further increases the amount of model parameters.

37
00:03:54,560 --> 00:04:00,440
In addition, we have experimentally verified the performance of different model architecture related hyperparameters.

38
00:04:01,470 --> 00:04:06,870
It ensure that a relatively lightweight network is used to obtain the best result of typhoon center location.

39
00:04:08,200 --> 00:04:14,600
We also analyze the impact of different standard deviations used to generate the ground-truth heatmap on the training results.

40
00:04:15,770 --> 00:04:20,410
Small standard deviation will lead to too steep probability value distribution of heatmap,

41
00:04:20,910 --> 00:04:25,590
which results in performance degradation due to too strict restrictions on the output of the model.

42
00:04:26,660 --> 00:04:35,180
Conversely, a too large standard deviation will increase the supervision error during the training process since the probability value distribution of heatmap is too flat.

43
00:04:36,120 --> 00:04:42,000
We conduct experiments on TCLNet with standard deviations ranging from 5 to 30 in interval of 5,

44
00:04:42,570 --> 00:04:46,770
and it can be seen that the best effect can be achieve when standard deviation is set to 15.

45
00:04:48,480 --> 00:04:51,400
Well, that's all of my presentation, thank you for your watching.
